<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph I, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1678">1678</a>–<a href="/years/1711">1711</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph I, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>, reigned from 1705 to 1711 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. After the allied victory at Blenheim he placed the Bavarian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> under the imperial ban and confiscated or redistributed their lands, while raising loyal princes in rank. He died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in 1711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-i-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Joseph I, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans van Stampart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JosephI.1705.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 26 July 1678, the eldest son of Leopold I from his third wife, Eleonor Magdalene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a>. He was educated strictly by Charles Theodore, Prince of Salm, and became a good linguist. Perhaps through the influence of his formerly Protestant tutor, he was a less devout Catholic than his parents and an adherent of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. His two enthusiasms were music and hunting. He was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> at the age of nine, in 1687, and elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at eleven. His only military service was at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> in 1702.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As emperor Joseph reduced the large number of privy councillors, tried to make the bureaucracy more efficient and had some success in stabilising the chronically poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> finances. He continued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> in an attempt to make his younger brother Charles king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Victories won by his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> established Austrian hegemony over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. When he laid claim to imperial rights in Italy, he risked a military conflict with the Pope over the Duchy of Mantua, and Pope Clement XI threatened him with excommunication on 16 June 1708.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> he inherited the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>, which lasted through his whole reign. Unlike his predecessors, he refrained from executing the leaders and agreed instead to a compromise peace, which in the long term eased the integration of Hungary into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> domains. In 1706 he confirmed the religious liberties of the Orthodox Serbs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>. In 1710 he extended his father's edict of outlawry against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>: those who returned after entering the realm were to be mutilated or branded, adult men hanged without trial, and women and boys flogged and banished. Mass killings were reported as a result.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic of 1711 Joseph became infected and died on 17 April at the Hofburg. He was buried in the Imperial Crypt, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> district of Josefstadt is named for him. He had married Wilhelmine Amalia of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> in 1699, and their only son died before his first birthday. Under the Mutual Pact of Succession, his daughters were to precede those of his brother Charles, a ruling that made no provision for Charles's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>. Neither the Spanish war nor the Hungarian revolt was resolved in his lifetime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joseph I, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Anthony Ulrich gave it to his younger son Louis Rudolph, and in 1707 <b>Emperor Joseph I</b> raised it to a principality, giving Louis Rudolph a seat among the princes of the Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-blankenburg">Principality of Blankenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Mindelheim, a Swabian lordship belonging to the Electorate of Bavaria, was confiscated by <b>Emperor Joseph I</b> after the Bavarian elector's defeat at Blenheim in 1704.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-mindelheim">Principality of Mindelheim</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1699" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Joseph I, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4231 5922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4231 5922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1699">What was here in 1699? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
